-      "F4",
-      "F6",
-      "F1",
-      "F5"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-2.1",
-     "claim": "If two distinct feasible points $p_1,p_2$ minimized $q$, their midpoint is feasible and $$q\\bigl((p_1+p_2)/2\\bigr)=\\tfrac12q(p_1)+\\tfrac12q(p_2)-\\tfrac18(p_1-p_2)^TB(p_1-p_2)<q(p_1),$$ contradicting minimality. Hence the minimizer $p_*$ is unique.",
-     "step": "2.1",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 2.1",
-      "step 1.1",
-      "A1"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-2.2",
-     "claim": "Let $I=\\{i:G_ip_*+d_i=0\\}$. For any $z$ with $Az=0$ and $G_iz\\le0$ for $i\\in I$, inactive constraints have positive slack, so for all sufficiently small $t>0$, $p_*+tz\\in C$. Minimality and the one-sided derivative at $t=0$ give $(Bp_*+c)^Tz\\ge0$. Therefore $v:=-(Bp_*+c)$ satisfies $v^Tz\\le0$ for every such $z$.",
-     "step": "2.2",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 2.2",
-      "A1",
-      "F1",
-      "step 1.1"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-3.1",
-     "claim": "If $v$ were not representable as $A^T\\eta+\\sum_{i\\in I}\\mu_iG_i^T$ with $\\mu_i\\ge0$, apply [F2] to the matrix whose columns are $G_i^T$ for $i\\in I$, the columns of $A^T$, and their negatives, with right-hand side $v$. The alternative gives $y$ with $G_iy\\ge0$, $Ay=0$, and $v^Ty<0$. Then $z=-y$ satisfies the direction conditions of step 2.2 but $v^Tz>0$, a contradiction. Thus $v=A^T\\eta+\\sum_{i\\in I}\\mu_iG_i^T$ for some $\\mu_i\\ge0$. Since $v=-(Bp_*+c)$, this is KKT stationarity with equality multiplier $\\eta$; setting inactive multipliers to zero gives complementarity.",
-     "step": "3.1",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 3.1",
-      "F2",
-      "step 2.2"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-4.1",
-     "claim": "If $A$ has positive row count, full row rank makes $AA^T$ positive definite, so set $P=I-A^T(AA^T)^{-1}A$; if it has zero rows, set $P=I$. Direct multiplication gives $AP=0$, $P^T=P$, and $P^2=P$, and $Pz=z$ for every $z\\in\\ker A$. Thus $P$ is the orthogonal projection onto $\\ker A$ and $\\ker P=\\operatorname{range}(A^T)$. Project the multiplier representation from step 3.1. If the projected active normals with positive coefficients are dependent, choose a nonzero dependence, change its sign to give a positive coefficient, and subtract $t$ times that relation, where $t$ is the minimum ratio of a current multiplier to a positive dependence coefficient. All coefficients remain nonnegative and at least one becomes zero. Repeating finitely many times leaves independent projected normals. Their unprojected remainder lies in $\\ker P=\\operatorname{range}(A^T)$, so it is absorbed into the equality multiplier. If a linear combination of the equality rows and supported active rows is zero, projecting it by $P$ first forces all active coefficients to be zero by projected independence; full row rank then forces the equality coefficients to be zero.",
-     "step": "4.1",
-     "inputs": [
