-      "given",
-      "step 1.1",
-      "F2",
-      "F1"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-1.2",
-     "claim": "The $x$-Hessian at the base point is $W_\\rho=H+\\rho A^TA$. If $r=0$, the assumed condition says $H\\succ0$. For $r>0$, choose orthonormal matrices $Z,Y$ spanning $\\ker A$ and its orthogonal complement, respectively; then $C_0=AY$ is invertible. In this basis the tangent block is $H_T=Z^THZ\\succ0$ when its dimension is positive, the cross blocks are those of $H$, and the normal block is $Y^THY+\\rho C_0^TC_0$. Its Schur complement over the tangent block is $D+\\rho C_0^TC_0$, where $D=Y^THY-Y^THZ H_T^{-1}Z^THY$; if the tangent block is empty, take $D=Y^THY$. Since $C_0^TC_0\\succ0$, this Schur complement is positive definite for sufficiently large $\\rho$. Hence $W_\\rho\\succ0$.",
-     "step": "1.2",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 1.2"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-2.1",
-     "claim": "If $r>0$, apply [F3] to $G(x,\\mu)=\\nabla_x\\Phi_\\rho(x,\\mu)=0$. Its $x$ derivative is $W_\\rho$, which is invertible by step 1.2, and its $\\mu$ derivative is $A^T$. Therefore there is a local $C^1$ branch $x(\\mu)$ with $x(\\mu_*)=x_*$ and $$Dx(\\mu_*)=-W_\\rho^{-1}A^T.$$ If $r=0$, the empty-multiplier branch consists of the local minimizer $x_*$. By continuity of the Hessian, after shrinking to a convex neighborhood $\\Phi_\\rho(\\cdot,\\mu)$ is uniformly strongly convex there; its stationary branch point is its unique local minimizer.",
-     "step": "2.1",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 2.1",
-      "F3",
-      "step 1.2"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-3.1",
-     "claim": "For $r>0$, the exact multiplier map is $T(\\mu)=\\mu+\\rho c(x(\\mu))$, so $$DT(\\mu_*)=I-\\rho A W_\\rho^{-1}A^T.$$ In the orthonormal coordinates of step 1.2, block elimination gives $A W_\\rho^{-1}A^T=C_0(D+\\rho C_0^TC_0)^{-1}C_0^T$ $=(\\rho I+E)^{-1}$, where $E=C_0^{-T}DC_0^{-1}$ is symmetric. Thus $DT(\\mu_*)=E(\\rho I+E)^{-1}$. Its eigenvalues are $e_i/(\\rho+e_i)$, which have absolute value less than $1$ for all sufficiently large $\\rho$. Continuity of $DT$ then gives a closed multiplier ball on which $T$ is a contraction with some factor $q<1$. For $r=0$ the multiplier update is absent.",
-     "step": "3.1",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 3.1",
-      "step 1.2",
-      "step 2.1"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-4.1",
-     "claim": "Let $\\widehat x_k$ be an inner point in the selected branch with residual at most $\\epsilon_k$, and let $x(\\mu_k)$ be the exact branch minimizer. Strong convexity with constant $\\alpha$ gives $$\\alpha\\|\\widehat x_k-x(\\mu_k)\\|_2^2\\le \\langle\\nabla\\Phi_\\rho(\\widehat x_k,\\mu_k)- \\nabla\\Phi_\\rho(x(\\mu_k),\\mu_k),\\widehat x_k-x(\\mu_k)\\rangle \\le\\epsilon_k\\|\\widehat x_k-x(\\mu_k)\\|_2.$$ After cancellation (with the zero-distance case immediate), $\\|\\widehat x_k-x(\\mu_k)\\|_2\\le\\epsilon_k/\\alpha$. The multiplier update is on the same PHR branch, so its difference from $T(\\mu_k)$ has norm at most $\\rho L_c\\epsilon_k/\\alpha=C\\epsilon_k$. Hence $$\\|\\mu_{k+1}-\\mu_*\\|_2\\le q\\|\\mu_k-\\mu_*\\|_2+C\\epsilon_k.$$ The stated sum bound keeps this recurrence inside the multiplier ball and keeps every inner point within the primal branch tube.",
-     "step": "4.1",
-     "inputs": [
-      "given",
-      "step 4.1",
-      "A1",
-      "F2",
-      "F4",
-      "step 2.1",
-      "step 3.1"
-     ]
-    },
-    {
-     "id": "derivation-5.1",
-     "claim": "Iterating the inequality gives $$\\|\\mu_k-\\mu_*\\|_2\\le q^k\\|\\mu_0-\\mu_*\\|_2+ C\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}q^{k-1-j}\\epsilon_j.$$ The first term tends to zero. For the sum, any fixed finite prefix tends to zero as $k\\to\\infty$, while the remaining tail is bounded by $C\\sum_{j\\ge J}\\epsilon_j$, which tends to zero as $J\\to\\infty$. Therefore $\\mu_k\\to\\mu_*$. Also $\\epsilon_k\\to0$ because it is summable, so step 4.1 gives $\\widehat x_k-x(\\mu_k)\\to0$ and continuity gives $\\widehat x_k\\to x_*$. When $r=0$, step 1.2 gives a strongly convex local objective and the same residual estimate directly yields $\\widehat x_k\\to x_*$.",
